[00:00.00]The chart above clearly demonstrates the total number of fans followers paying close attention to the Microblog of entertainment stars from 2017 to 2018.
[00:13.23]In 2017,
[00:13.94]the number was at a low level of 12.8 billion,
[00:17.71]while within one year,
[00:19.65]it reached 16.7 billion,
[00:21.78]a rise of 3.9 billion.
[00:23.62]Undoubtedly,
[00:24.84]the diagram demonstrates the social phenomenon of idol worship,
[00:30.44]which is prevalent among teenagers nowadays.
[00:34.41]Briefly speaking,
[00:36.04]young people areinclined to idolize those who excel in appearance or talent.
[00:42.66]For instance,
[00:43.88]Su Bingtian represents the image of a sports hero,
[00:48.16]whose handsome appearance and unparalleled skills are passionately adored by his sports fans.
[00:56.40]By contrast,
[00:57.52]several teenage boys and girls win their fame overnight in a great many contests on TV or online,
[01:05.67]which provokes noisy nationwide debates on the value and harm of admiring these idols.
[01:13.10]In my opinion,
[01:14.32]idolatry is a double-edged sword which can exert a profound influence for either good or bad on the growth of young people.
[01:24.71]If we endeavor to improve ourselves by bridging the gap between our role models and ourselves,
[01:32.75]we will lead a positive and fruitful life.
